<section class="cp-topic" id="methodology-changelog">
<h2>Methodology changelog</h2>
<p>Each profile shows its methodology version ("Version <em>N</em>") on its About this data slide. The version is a monotonic integer, not a date: it increments only when a pipeline change could move a figure while the underlying source data stays the same (switching the intersect method, changing the rollup geography, fixing an aggregation bug), never for a new section, a restyle or an added county. Two snapshots carrying different versions are never joined by a trend line.</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
<div><dt>Version 1</dt><dd>The version in development. Orange County was first computed from real data in Phase 2 (block-level areal apportionment of people and jobs to the flood and sea level rise extents); Phase 3 extended the same method to all 27 counties (FEMA flood zones found through the political-jurisdiction layer, sea level rise polygons read from every NOAA regional file a county touches, each county measured on a projection centred on it). The version stays 1 until the tool is published; from then on any change that could move a figure increments it.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Phase 4 (still version 1)</dt><dd>Sea level rise sections are framed against the OPC scenarios: the projected rise at 2050 and 2100 for each recommended scenario, the full Appendix F table on this page, and every snapshot carrying its gauge's thirteen decades. No exposure figure, and no crossing year, moved; the version stays 1.</dd></div>
</dl>
</section>
